---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';
import { SCALE } from '../../../../config/scalability.mjs';

const title = 'I am confused about my career after 10th: what to decide now and what can wait';
const seoTitle = 'I Am Confused About My Career After 10th: What to Decide First';
const description =
  'Confused about your career after 10th? You do not need a career yet, only one route for Class 11 and 12 that keeps doors open. Here is how to pick it.';
const seoDescription = description;
const canonical =
  'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-guidance/i-am-confused-about-my-career-after-10th/';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-11-15';
const publishedDateLabel = '15 November 2026';
const authorName = 'Shivanshi Sehgal';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';
const salesHref =
  '/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-after-10th/';
const salesLabel = 'Get Career Counselling After 10th';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'decide-now', label: 'What you really have to decide now' },
  { id: 'this-week', label: 'Do this week' },
  { id: 'kinds', label: 'Which kind of confused are you?' },
  { id: 'doors', label: 'What each route keeps open, closes and how far it can grow' },
  { id: 'test', label: 'Test a route before you commit' },
  { id: 'checkpoint', label: 'Check before you commit' },
  { id: 'parents', label: 'Talking to your parents' },
  { id: 'low-marks', label: 'If your marks are low' },
  { id: 'money', label: 'Money check before you pay' },
  { id: 'portfolio', label: 'Build a high-income skill portfolio beside your route' },
  { id: 'heavier', label: 'When it feels heavier than confusion' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Mistakes that cost a year' },
  { id: 'next-step', label: 'What to do next' },
  { id: 'read-next', label: 'Read next' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const kinds = [
  {
    name: 'I like many things and cannot narrow down',
    going: 'At 15 you have seen only a few kinds of work. Liking many things is normal, and it is not a flaw.',
    move: 'Keep two routes, not one. Do a real sample task from each and note which one you were still doing after the task was over.',
    watch: 'Waiting for total certainty. It rarely arrives before Class 11 starts.',
  },
  {
    name: 'My parents want the safe stream',
    going: 'Safe usually means a stream with a job they can name at the end. Their fear is real, and so is yours.',
    move: 'Bring one page: two routes, what each keeps open, the cost and your backup. Ask them to read it with you.',
    watch: 'Arguing about the stream label. Argue about evidence instead.',
  },
  {
    name: 'All my friends are taking science',
    going: 'When you cannot see your own answer, the group answer feels safe.',
    move: 'Ask three friends why they chose it. Then write your own reason in one line. If you cannot, you are copying.',
    watch: 'Choosing two years of subjects for company. You will sit in the subjects, not in the group chat.',
  },
  {
    name: 'I am good at one subject but cannot see the work it leads to',
    going: 'A subject is not a job. The same subject feeds many very different kinds of work.',
    move: 'Find five jobs that use that subject. Read what people in them do all day, and talk to one of them.',
    watch: 'Assuming a high mark in a subject means the work will suit you.',
  },
  {
    name: 'I am in a coaching batch and losing interest',
    going: 'Many students join a batch before they have chosen a goal, and the batch then chooses for them.',
    move: 'Ask yourself if you want the exam or only the batch. Read the refund rules before you decide anything.',
    watch: 'Staying because the fee is paid. The fee is gone either way, and the next two years are still yours.',
  },
  {
    name: 'My school was in a regional language and English scares me',
    going: 'Language discomfort is not low ability. English matters because it widens options, not because it measures intelligence.',
    move: 'Choose the route by the work first. Then build English around real work: read a little each day and explain one project aloud.',
    watch: 'Dropping a route only because the textbooks look hard in English.',
  },
  {
    name: 'I am scared I will pick the wrong one',
    going: 'Every route looks permanent from where you stand. Most of them are not, and the table below shows which ones are hard to leave.',
    move: 'Read the last column of that table: how easy is it to change your mind on each route? Prefer the one you can still leave cheaply.',
    watch: 'Treating a two-year choice as a lifetime verdict. That fear is what freezes people.',
  },
  {
    name: 'My family cannot afford a costly or long route',
    going: 'Some routes need years of paid coaching, a hostel in another city or a private seat. A thin family budget changes which route is safe.',
    move: 'Add up the full cost of each route to the first job, not only the first year. Put a cheaper route on the list with the same care as the famous one.',
    watch: 'Borrowing for a coaching or college fee before you have tested the route.',
  },
];

const doorRows = [
  {
    route: 'Science with Maths (PCM)',
    open: 'Engineering (B.Tech or B.E.), computing, B.Sc, architecture, data and most non-medical degrees.',
    closes: 'MBBS, BDS and other NEET medical courses, which need Biology. Veterinary science and some life-science degrees also ask for Biology.',
    change: 'Most commerce and humanities degrees do not ask for Science, so moving later is mostly a question of the school rule. Ask in writing.',
    scale: 'mid',
    why: 'Computing and data can grow into your own products or consulting. A branch degree alone is a crowded ladder, so add one project that people use.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Commerce',
    open: 'BCom, BBA, CA, CS, CMA, economics and law, where CLAT accepts any stream.',
    closes: 'Engineering and NEET medical courses.',
    change: 'Moving into science after Class 11 starts is hard because the syllabus has already moved on.',
    scale: 'mid',
    why: 'Finance, business and data work grow when you add a skill such as spreadsheets, selling or analysis. CA or a general degree on its own is crowded.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Humanities',
    open: 'BA subjects, law, psychology, journalism, teaching, design routes and civil services preparation alongside a degree.',
    closes: 'Engineering, medical courses and most B.Sc science degrees.',
    change: 'Many arts degrees ask for no fixed subject. Moving into science later is hard.',
    scale: 'mid',
    why: 'Design, writing, media and psychology grow when you own a portfolio or an audience. Law and civil services are long ladders with few winners.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Diploma, ITI or open school (NIOS)',
    open: 'Work or training earlier. Diploma holders can often enter the second year of B.Tech through lateral entry. NIOS senior secondary counts as Class 12.',
    closes: 'For diploma and ITI, the direct school route to NEET or JEE, which need specific Class 12 subjects. With NIOS those doors stay open if you pick the right subjects.',
    change: 'Lateral entry has marks conditions (AICTE\'s 2022 rule asked for 45 percent in the diploma, 40 percent for SC and ST candidates), and NIOS lets you choose subjects. Check both on the official page before you decide.',
    scale: 'mid',
    why: 'A diploma or a trade follows a fixed ladder at first. Income has more room to grow when you add digital skills, or later run your own repair, installation or technical service.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Science with Biology (PCB)',
    open: 'NEET medical courses, nursing, pharmacy, life science and psychology routes.',
    closes: 'Engineering through JEE, which needs Maths. Pick this route only if you really like Biology, accept a long and competitive training path, can fund it and keep a backup plan.',
    change: 'Adding Maths later is hard once Class 11 has started. If both doors matter, ask whether the school offers PCMB, which is the heaviest workload.',
    scale: 'low',
    why: 'The best-known doors, MBBS and BDS, mean competition twice: for a NEET seat first, then for postgraduate seats, and income comes late. The doors with more room to grow are health-tech, nutrition and data, so keep a second route.',
  },
];

const testSteps = [
  {
    title: 'Cut your list to two routes',
    body: 'Write every route you have heard of, then remove the ones you can already say no to. Keep two that you cannot yet rule out.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Do a real sample of each route',
    body: 'Solve a full chapter of Class 11 Maths or Biology. Read a commerce basics chapter and try a small budget sheet. Visit an ITI workshop or watch a diploma student explain their lab. Sample work is better than opinions.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Talk to two people who live the route',
    body: 'Ask one student a year ahead what the first term is really like. Ask one adult doing the work what the boring 80 percent of the day looks like.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Note your energy, not your marks',
    body: 'After each sample, write one line: did I want to continue, or did I want it to end? Your energy tells you more than a first-try score.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Write a decision note with a review date',
    body: 'Write the route, the reason, the cost, the backup and the date you will review. Work backwards from your school or state admission date, because that deadline is real even when your pace is your own.',
  },
];

const checks = [
  {
    title: 'Your fit: what kind of day suits you?',
    body: 'Some people like solving problems step by step. Some like remembering and explaining. Some like working with hands, others with people or numbers. Try a small task from each route and notice which one you finish.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Your situation: what can your family carry for two years and the stage after?',
    body: 'Look at fees, travel, coaching and the city the next stage may need. A route you cannot afford to finish is not a safe route, whatever its name.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Job market: where does each route lead in real life?',
    body: 'Class 10 is early for jobs but not early for looking. Read five real job posts for one job you like and note the degree, the skills and the entry level they ask for.',
  },
  {
    title: 'AI-proof: which part of the work still needs a person?',
    body: 'No route is safe just by its name. Routine tasks such as basic coding, data entry and first drafts are the first ones tools take over. Ask which part of the route needs judgment, hands-on skill, talking to people or checking a tool\'s answer, and use AI now to explain a hard chapter, then check it against your book.',
  },
];

const proofs = [
  {
    num: 'Proof 1',
    name: 'Show it',
    body: 'Finish one small thing in the direction you lean: a solved chapter with your own notes, a family budget sheet, a repaired gadget or a one-page website.',
  },
  {
    num: 'Proof 2',
    name: 'Say it',
    body: 'Explain your route and your reason to a parent in 30 seconds to 2 minutes, with no notes. If you cannot, the decision is not ready.',
  },
  {
    num: 'Proof 3',
    name: 'Get it checked',
    body: 'Show your work to a teacher, a senior or a person doing that work. Write down what they say is missing.',
  },
];

const skillSteps = [
  {
    title: 'Whatever your route: build the digital floor',
    body: 'Learn to type well, use documents, slides and a spreadsheet, search with care and use an AI tool to check your thinking, not to replace it. English reading and speaking sit here too.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Next: finish one small project in the direction you lean',
    body: 'One finished project beats five started ones. It can be a simple page, a data chart in a sheet, a short explainer video or a school event you ran well.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Later: make the work visible and get feedback',
    body: 'Show it to people who do the work and ask what is weak. Over time this proof, plus communication and the right skill mix, is what moves a person toward a high-income skill portfolio. No route guarantees it.',
  },
];

const mistakes = [
  {
    title: 'Treating the stream as the whole career',
    body: 'A stream is a two-year learning environment. The career decision comes later and has several more doors in it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Waiting for certainty until the admission list closes',
    body: 'Confusion that you never test only gets older. A decision with a review date is better than no decision.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Letting one number choose for you',
    body: 'One mark, one rank or one online test is a single signal. Use it beside real samples and real conversations.',
  },
];

const readNext = [
  { href: '/blog/stream-selection/career-options-after-10th/', text: 'Career options after 10th', why: 'for the full map of science, commerce, humanities, diploma and ITI routes.' },
  { href: '/blog/stream-selection/career-chart-after-10th/', text: 'Career chart after 10th', why: 'for a printable chart of each route and the years it takes to reach work.' },
  { href: '/blog/stream-selection/which-stream-to-choose-in-11th/', text: 'Which stream to choose in 11th', why: 'for the subject-level decision once you have a route in mind.' },
  { href: '/blog/career-guidance/how-to-choose-a-career-when-you-have-no-interests-india/', text: 'How to choose a career when you have no interests', why: 'if nothing seems to pull you at all.' },
  { href: '/blog/college-degrees/courses-for-weak-students-after-10th-india/', text: 'Courses for students with low marks after 10th', why: 'for routes that work when marks are the worry.' },
  { href: '/blog/parents/signs-of-career-confusion-in-teenager-india/', text: 'Signs of career confusion in a teenager', why: 'if a parent wants to understand what you are going through.' },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Is it normal to be confused about my career after 10th?',
    a: 'Yes. Most students at 15 have seen only a few kinds of work, so a lack of clarity is normal. The way out is to test two routes with real samples and real conversations, not to wait for a feeling.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I have to choose my career right after 10th?',
    a: 'No. You have to choose a route for Class 11 and 12 (a science group, commerce, humanities, diploma, ITI or open school). The career choice comes later, and several more decisions sit between the two.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which stream should I take if I am completely confused?',
    a: 'There is no stream that is right for everyone. If you can handle Maths and have no clear Biology goal, science with Maths keeps the most degrees open, but it closes medical, dental and veterinary courses. If you cannot sustain the Maths and Physics workload, forcing it costs two years, so test a chapter first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it possible to switch streams once Class 11 has started?',
    a: 'Some schools and boards allow a change early in Class 11, and many do not. Ask for the rule in writing before you pay fees, and treat a change as a backup, not a plan.',
  },
  {
    q: 'My parents want a different stream from the one I like. What do I do?',
    a: 'Bring a one-page note with two routes, what each keeps open, the cost and a backup. Ask them to read it with you, then agree on a review date.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a diploma or ITI a good choice if I am confused?',
    a: 'It is a real route, not a lesser one, if you like practical work and want to reach a job earlier. Diploma holders can often enter the second year of B.Tech through lateral entry, with marks conditions, so check the current AICTE and state notices.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can an online career test tell me what to choose after 10th?',
    a: 'A test shows patterns in your answers, not your future. Use the result as one signal, then check it with a real sample task and a talk with someone who does the work.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I join a coaching batch to help me decide?',
    a: 'Coaching does not choose a direction for you. Test your direction first, ask for a trial, and read the refund rules before paying.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I take open schooling (NIOS) after 10th?',
    a: 'Yes. NIOS offers a senior secondary course for students who passed Class 10, equal to Class 12, with a choice of subjects and admission available online through the year. Check the current rules on the official NIOS site.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should I ask for professional help?',
    a: 'Ask when you have tested two routes and still cannot decide, or when the family disagreement is blocking the choice. If the stress feels heavy, talk to a trusted adult or call Tele-MANAS on 14416, which is free and open 24 hours a day.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  description={description}
  seoDescription={seoDescription}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-guidance"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  salesHref={salesHref}
  salesLabel={salesLabel}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>If you keep thinking "I am confused about my career after 10th", you are not behind: you do not have to choose a career yet, only one route for Class 11 and 12, such as science, commerce, humanities, diploma or ITI.</strong>
    Choose it so that most doors stay open. The decisions that unlock high income opportunities come later, and they are built on skills you can show.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Answer:</strong> Pick a route, not a career. Keep two routes in play and test each with a real sample task and one real conversation.</li>
      <li><strong>Filter:</strong> For each route ask what it keeps open, what it closes, and how easy it is to change your mind. Choose the one you can sustain for two years.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> Write a one-page decision note with a review date. A high-income skill portfolio built beside your route can help you reach earlier financial freedom, but no route promises it.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Confusion at this age usually comes from too little information and too much pressure, not from a lack of ability. Both can be fixed with a plain process.
  </p>

  <p>
    When you have a short list of routes, the free <a href="/services/assessments/stream-selector-test-after-10th/">stream selector test after 10th</a> shows which Class 11 stream fits you best.
  </p>

  <p class="more-guides">
    Want someone to go through your marks, your family and your options with you? <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-after-10th/">Career counselling after 10th</a> does that, and the <a href="/blog/career-guidance/">Career Guidance section</a> has more guides like this one.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="decide-now">I am confused about my career after 10th: what you really have to decide now</h2>

  <p>
    Most of the weight you feel comes from treating three decisions as one: your route, your college course and your career. Only the route is due now.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Due now</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Your route for Class 11 and 12: a science group, commerce, humanities, diploma, ITI or open school.</li>
        <li>Your subjects inside that route.</li>
        <li>A backup you could move to if the first choice does not work.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Can wait</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Your final career and job title.</li>
        <li>Your college course, which you pick after Class 12 with better information.</li>
        <li>The branch you pick inside your course, your city and your first employer.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The expensive wrong turn is not choosing slowly. It is paying a long coaching fee, or locking a route that closes the door you actually want, before you have tested anything.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A route is a learning environment for two years. It is not an identity, and it does not decide your income. What you build beside it decides far more.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="this-week">Do this week</h2>
  <p>You do not need a final career this week. You need two routes written down and one honest test of each. Write every answer on one page, because that page becomes your decision note.</p>
  <div class="week-box">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Day 1.</strong> Write the two routes you are weighing, such as science and commerce, or Class 11 and a diploma. Under each, write one thing it closes and one thing it keeps open.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 2.</strong> Ask your school for your subject-wise marks. Then read the entry rule for both routes in the official notice for your own board and state, not in a coaching advertisement.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 3 and 4.</strong> Try one real sample from each route. For science, solve five problems from a Class 11 book; for commerce, write a day of income and spending for a small shop.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 5.</strong> Talk to one person who took each route, such as a senior, a relative or a teacher. Ask what they would do differently.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 6 and 7.</strong> Write the decision note: your route, your backup and the date you will review it with your parents. If the two routes still feel equal, take the free <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments/stream-selector-test-after-10th/">stream selector test after 10th</a>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="week-box__note">This does not replace a talk with a school counsellor or teacher who knows your marks. Do not pay a coaching fee this week.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="kinds">Which kind of confused are you?</h2>

  <p>
    Confusion has different causes, and each one needs a different first move. OECD's Dream Jobs? report, launched in January 2020, found that about half of teenagers expected to work in just one of ten popular jobs, so seeing more kinds of work is often the real fix.
  </p>

  <div class="route-grid">
    {kinds.map((item, i) => (
      <div class="route-card">
        <div class="route-card__top"><span class="route-card__n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span></div>
        <h3>{item.name}</h3>
        <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What is going on</span>{item.going}</p>
        <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>First move</span>{item.move}</p>
        <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>{item.watch}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="doors">What each route keeps open, what it closes and how far it can grow</h2>

  <p>
    Compare routes by their doors, not by their prestige. Rules differ by board, state and school, so confirm each point on the official page before you pay.
  </p>

  <p>
    Science with Maths and Commerce come first because their doors reach computing, data, finance and business work, where added skill can raise income fastest. Science with Biology comes last because its best-known doors are narrow seat ladders.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Route comparison, scrollable table">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Keeps open</th>
          <th>Closes</th>
          <th>If you change your mind</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {doorRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{row.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Keeps open">{row.open}</td>
            <td data-label="Closes">{row.closes}</td>
            <td data-label="If you change your mind">{row.change}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    NIOS details are from the official <a href="https://nios.ac.in/student-information-section/admission-procedure.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIOS admission page</a>. The 45 and 40 percent lateral entry marks are from a Careers360 report of AICTE's rules dated 9 March 2022, so check the current notice. Veterinary eligibility is from the <a href="https://www.vci.dahd.gov.in/sites/default/files/News%20update/CompositIBAY2026-27.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Veterinary Council of India booklet for 2026-27</a> and the CLAT rule from the <a href="https://consortiumofnlus.ac.in/clat-2027/ug-eligibility.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Consortium of NLUs</a>, checked on 16 November 2026.
  </p>

  <h3>How far each route can grow</h3>

  <p>
    We rate a route Scalable, Scalable with a business layer or Not so scalable by how far your income and reach can grow through skills, consulting or a business of your own. No route earns the top rating from its name alone.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Route scalability, scrollable table">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Our rating</th>
          <th>Why, and what to add</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {doorRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{row.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Our rating"><strong class={`scale-tag scale-tag--${row.scale}`}>{SCALE[row.scale].label}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Why, and what to add">{row.why}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>If you are truly undecided and can handle Maths,</strong> science with Maths is the broader default because it keeps engineering, computing, data and most degrees open. It is not for everyone: it closes medicine, dentistry and veterinary courses, and two years of Maths and Physics you cannot sustain is a poor trade.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Whichever route you pick, the decisions that unlock high income opportunities come from the skills and proof you add beside it, and they lead toward earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="test">Test a route before you commit</h2>

  <p>
    A short test beats months of worry. Some students finish it in a few weeks and others need a couple of months, and both are fine as long as you beat your admission date.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">What the first term feels like</p>
    <p>
      Class 11 moves faster than Class 10. A science group means regular problem practice and practicals, commerce means steady reading, numbers and accounts, and humanities means a lot of reading and writing. A student who has already tried one chapter of the route knows what the weekly work feels like before the fee is paid.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="ladder">
    {testSteps.map((step, i) => (
      <div class="ladder-step">
        <div class="ladder-step__num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
        <div>
          <div class="ladder-step__title">{step.title}</div>
          <p>{step.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--gold">
    <p>
      <strong>If two routes still feel equal,</strong> pick the one that costs less to leave, keeps more doors open and gives you a better backup. A tie is a sign that either choice can work, so the cost of leaving is what should decide it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Check before you commit</h2>

  <p>
    Run the <strong>four questions</strong> on each route you are still holding, then pass the <strong>three proofs</strong> before you treat a route as final.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol">
    {checks.map((item, i) => (
      <div class="protocol-row">
        <div class="protocol-row__num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
